aarch64: don't reach Thread::_thr_current through TLSDESC on android

threadLS_linux_aarch64 reads the current thread out of a TLS slot with the
TLSDESC sequence:

  adrp x0, :tlsdesc:_ZN6Thread12_thr_currentE
  .tlsdesccall _ZN6Thread12_thr_currentE

bionic's linker does not support TLSDESC relocations at the API level built
here, so the reference cannot be resolved and libjvm.so is left with an
undefined Thread::_thr_current. Shared libraries tolerate that at link time,
which is why it stays invisible -- until something links an executable against
libjvm.so:

  ld.lld: error: undefined reference due to --no-allow-shlib-undefined:
      Thread::_thr_current

11 is the release that surfaced it, because it bundles googletest and builds
gtestLauncher; 17 and up need --with-gtest and never link such a binary. The
relocation is identical in all of them, so all of them get the fix.

Drop the assembly on android and let aarch64_get_thread_helper() return
Thread::current() instead, as termux-packages does in
0006-aarch64_get_thread_helper-Thread-current and
0007-Remove-aarch64_get_thread_helper.

11 needs the exclusion done in the makefile: its copy is threadLS_linux_aarch64.s
with a lowercase extension, so the compiler never preprocesses it and it cannot
guard itself. The gate is the OPENJDK_TARGET_LIBC test patch 0011 already uses.

CompileGtest.gmk needs the same exclusion spelled out again: it builds its own
copy of libjvm and hardcodes EXCLUDE_FILES to gtestLauncher.cpp rather than
inheriting JVM_EXCLUDE_FILES, so the first attempt at this patch fixed the real
libjvm.so and left the gtest one still assembling the TLSDESC sequence -- which
is the copy gtestLauncher links against, so the build failed in exactly the
same place.

diff --git a/make/hotspot/lib/CompileGtest.gmk b/make/hotspot/lib/CompileGtest.gmk
index f6af4d3..bb37a23 100644
--- a/make/hotspot/lib/CompileGtest.gmk
+++ b/make/hotspot/lib/CompileGtest.gmk
@@ -66,7 +66,7 @@ $(eval $(call SetupNativeCompilation, BUILD_GTEST_LIBJVM, \
     OBJECT_DIR := $(JVM_OUTPUTDIR)/gtest/objs, \
     SRC := $(GTEST_TEST_SRC), \
     EXCLUDES := $(JVM_EXCLUDES), \
-    EXCLUDE_FILES := gtestLauncher.cpp, \
+    EXCLUDE_FILES := gtestLauncher.cpp $(JVM_EXCLUDE_FILES), \
     EXCLUDE_PATTERNS := $(JVM_EXCLUDE_PATTERNS), \
     EXTRA_FILES := $(GTEST_FRAMEWORK_SRC)/src/gtest-all.cc, \
     EXTRA_OBJECT_FILES := $(filter-out %/operator_new$(OBJ_SUFFIX), \
diff --git a/make/hotspot/lib/CompileJvm.gmk b/make/hotspot/lib/CompileJvm.gmk
index 48e75e2..8aca7bc 100644
--- a/make/hotspot/lib/CompileJvm.gmk
+++ b/make/hotspot/lib/CompileJvm.gmk
@@ -55,6 +55,15 @@ JVM_LIBS += \
 
 # These files and directories are always excluded
 JVM_EXCLUDE_FILES += args.cc
+
+# threadLS_linux_aarch64.s reads Thread::_thr_current through TLSDESC, which
+# bionic's linker cannot resolve; the header supplies a plain Thread::current()
+# in its place there. The file is .s rather than .S, so it is never run through
+# the preprocessor and cannot guard itself. android is the linux target whose
+# libc configure reports as neither gnu nor musl.
+ifeq (197121call isTargetOs, linux)-197121if 197121filter gnu musl, 197121OPENJDK_TARGET_LIBC)),known,other), true-other)
+  JVM_EXCLUDE_FILES += threadLS_linux_aarch64.s
+endif
 JVM_EXCLUDES += adlc
 
 # Needed by abstract_vm_version.cpp
diff --git a/src/hotspot/os_cpu/linux_aarch64/thread_linux_aarch64.hpp b/src/hotspot/os_cpu/linux_aarch64/thread_linux_aarch64.hpp
index 985b664..ee69e20 100644
--- a/src/hotspot/os_cpu/linux_aarch64/thread_linux_aarch64.hpp
+++ b/src/hotspot/os_cpu/linux_aarch64/thread_linux_aarch64.hpp
@@ -60,7 +60,16 @@ private:
   bool pd_get_top_frame(frame* fr_addr, void* ucontext, bool isInJava);
 public:
 
-  static Thread *aarch64_get_thread_helper();
+  // bionic's linker cannot resolve the TLSDESC relocations the assembly below
+  // uses to read Thread::_thr_current, so read the thread the ordinary way.
+  static Thread *aarch64_get_thread_helper()
+#ifndef __ANDROID__
+;
+#else
+  {
+    return Thread::current();
+  }
+#endif
 
   // These routines are only used on cpu architectures that
   // have separate register stacks (Itanium).
